  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p>At 14,259 feet, <strong>Longs Peak</strong> is a summit in Colorado. The Longs Peak Trail covers 5.0 miles with about 2,421 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a steady climb. It's rated Moderate overall.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>The grade is back-loaded: the first half of the route climbs gently, and most of the elevation gain comes in the second half. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 3.59, gaining roughly 800 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 9,734 feet, the route tops out near 12,090 feet. You'll cross above treeline on the way up, so the upper route is exposed to weather \u2014 check the forecast and turn around if conditions build.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=40.31034,-105.64037\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">40.31034, -105.64037</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 50 miles northwest of Denver, Colorado. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>The reliable snow-free window is roughly July through September. At this elevation, afternoon thunderstorms are a serious summer hazard \u2014 start early and plan to be off the upper mountain by midday. Outside that window expect snow travel and bring traction and navigation skills to match.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Moderate rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 3.0 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Torreys Peak, Grays Peak and Mount Blue Sky \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
